WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.089 --> 00:00:07.689
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: during the course of the
agenda this morning.

00:00:07.689 --> 00:00:12.220
So shall we move on, and in accordance with
the rearrangement that we agreed earlier:

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:18.560
Arvind, you have had your cue from Simon,
may I invite you to talk to the paper on general

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:19.650
practice services?

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:24.310
&gt;&gt;Arvind Madan: Thank you, Malcolm.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:31.050
The purpose of the paper is to provide an
update on progress to date and the key next

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.050
steps.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.960
By way of background, we are all acutely aware
of the pressures general practice is facing

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.239
from rising demand and case complexity.

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:44.280
Whilst patient satisfaction remains high,
at 85.2% of the public reporting a good experience,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:50.490
this does mask a number of significant challenges
around recruitment and morale and other areas.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:55.670
The General Practice Forward View was published
on 21st April and includes 80 practical, funded

00:00:55.670 --> 00:01:02.809
commitments grouped into five key areas of
focus: investment, workforce, workload, infrastructure

00:01:02.809 --> 00:01:03.809
and care redesign.

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:09.100
It is a five year programme that sets out
an increasing investment profile, culminating

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:16.780
in a recurrent £2.4 billion per annum by
2021, aimed at both strengthening general

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.770
practice in the short term and supporting
a more sustainable transformation of primary

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.409
care in the future.

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:27.719
It builds on emerging solutions around self
care, working at scale, use of technology,

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:32.569
use of the skills and capabilities of both
the clinical and non clinical workforce.

00:01:32.569 --> 00:01:39.749
So, in the last 14 weeks since it was launched
on 21st April, I would like to share with

00:01:39.749 --> 00:01:44.719
colleagues some of the things that have been
happening.

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:48.669
We have already started delivering against
some of those 80 commitments: a new scheme

00:01:48.669 --> 00:01:53.899
to help doctors returning back into general
practice, including relocation costs and educational

00:01:53.899 --> 00:02:00.390
bursaries; we have begun the procurement exercise
of the service to help GPs facing stress and

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:07.460
burn out, and aim to have this service available
nationally and up and running in December;

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:13.790
we have started processing CCG applications
for the Primary Care Estates and Transformation

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:19.129
Fund, and that will establish a pipeline of
investment in primary care.

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:23.930
In addition, the NHS Property Services standard
lease has been agreed, which includes a commitment

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.849
to fund stamp duty and land tax for practices,
underpinned by an investment of £7 million.

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:36.709
We have established the GP IT operating model,
which is back by an 18% increase in CCG allocations

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:41.659
for the provision of IT services and technology
for general practice.

00:02:41.659 --> 00:02:50.180
On workload, today we will be issuing a letter
jointly with NHS Improvement, detailing out

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:57.569
the responsibilities to NHS Trusts and CCGs
in relation to the changes in the standard

00:02:57.569 --> 00:03:03.300
contract made in 2016/17, and reminding them
of the responsibilities around this.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.909
This is particularly important as it will
reduce the number of unnecessary patient journeys

00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:12.749
to hospital, and also some avoidable workload
for practices.

00:03:12.749 --> 00:03:18.599
We are also establishing a new multi stakeholder
primary care interface group, which will drive

00:03:18.599 --> 00:03:24.969
efficiencies in the current working arrangements
between general practice and hospitals.

00:03:24.969 --> 00:03:32.569
In terms of next steps, the first £16 million
of the four year £40 million general practice

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:37.799
resilience programme is being devolved to
local area teams to help struggling practices

00:03:37.799 --> 00:03:40.569
across the country now.

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:44.499
This programme builds on the work that is
already underway in relation to the vulnerable

00:03:44.499 --> 00:03:51.909
practices programme, but the scope will be
increased so that actually we can mobilise

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:57.469
rapid intervention for practices at risk of
closure; a wider menu of support options that

00:03:57.469 --> 00:04:05.450
are available to more practices and going
upstream when problems arise; helping with

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:10.700
workforce issues and engaging experienced
clinical capacity into those local health

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:17.310
economies and helping them develop skill mix
solutions; and actually working beyond individual

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:23.040
practices, with groups of practices in troubled
areas, to prevent, when one practice is struggling,

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.740
the domino effect of others coming under significant
pressure; and removing the requirement for

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:33.900
matched funding from practices to engage with
this support.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:39.509
We are also releasing details of the three
year, £30 million general practice development

00:04:39.509 --> 00:04:43.740
programme, which will eventually give every
practice in the country, over a three year

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:49.300
period, the opportunity to benefit from the
organisational development support contained

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.650
in that programme.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:56.419
That will mean running local time for care
programmes in each area that last 9 12 months,

00:04:56.419 --> 00:05:04.000
with intense workshops and a variety of other
engagement activities, that include rolling

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.340
out the ten high impact initiatives, which
were innovations that were developed during

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:15.050
the general practice access fund schemes,
with an ambition of eventually releasing 10%

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.520
of clinical time for GPs.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.419
Linked to this, we are setting out our plans
for training receptionists and clerical staff

00:05:23.419 --> 00:05:28.920
to play a greater role in navigation of patients
through the system and handling clinical paperwork

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:36.710
burden within practices, and plans for the
online consultation system and uptake of that.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:40.540
We have also been working with the Department
of Health and the profession, and medical

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.340
defence organisations, to consider proposals
on indemnity.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:49.919
Now, this is a complex area, so we are taking
a twin track approach to how we are dealing

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:50.919
with this.

00:05:50.919 --> 00:05:56.860
Firstly, to alleviate the immediate pressures,
we will introduce a new indemnity support

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:05.340
scheme for practices for at least the next
two years, and this will build on the work

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.669
that has already happened in relation to last
year's GP contract where an investment of

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:15.919
£33 million was made into the contract to
support this particular pressure, with a similar

00:06:15.919 --> 00:06:25.050
amount in the coming year payable in April
2017 to practices, and again in April 2018.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:31.139
The area of out of hours indemnity cost is
particularly complex, and work will be carrying

00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:35.930
on this year to establish a way forward.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:40.759
However, as a holding arrangement, we will
continue the winter indemnity scheme for this

00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:43.090
year.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:48.410
Given the complexity of this particular field,
alongside this we will continue work with

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:52.289
the Department of Health and others, including
the NHS Litigation Authority, to look at the

00:06:52.289 --> 00:06:58.199
scope for more fundamental reform in the system
during this period.

00:06:58.199 --> 00:07:02.380
On the retaining doctors scheme, we are increasing
the allowances payable to both the individual

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:05.430
doctors and the practices hosting them.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:09.409
In addition, on workforce, we are working
with Health Education England to increase

00:07:09.409 --> 00:07:14.639
the pool of GP applicants, particularly from
medical schools across Europe that train in

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:19.909
English, and where a lot of the students are
in fact UK citizens.

00:07:19.909 --> 00:07:25.669
The board will be reviewing a separate paper
shortly on the multi speciality community

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:31.310
provider framework, but just to say that we
are working with the NAPC, the National Association

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:36.690
of Primary Care, on building on their primary
care home initiative in the coming year, over

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:39.490
and above the 15 sites they already have running.

00:07:39.490 --> 00:07:48.110
We will be commissioning list validation work
with a view to investing any savings that

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:51.650
may be made back into general practice.

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:58.319
Lastly, we have established an external oversight
group, including the Royal College of GPs,

00:07:58.319 --> 00:08:03.569
the General Practitioners Committee, NHS Clinical
Commissioners, the Queen's Institute of Nursing

00:08:03.569 --> 00:08:11.110
and National Voices to give us some overall
steer and oversight into the activities that

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:15.300
we are rolling out through the General Practice
Forward View, and that is supported by the

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:21.990
Royal College of GPs, having recently employed
29 regional ambassadors to support that work

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.889
in each of the areas.

00:08:23.889 --> 00:08:28.640
We have started a reference group, with the
LMCs from around the country, where we can

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.300
have rapid feedback on whether the changes
that we are rolling out through the Forward

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.380
View are having an impact.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.830
So I will stop there.

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:38.010
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Great, thank you very much,
Arvind.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:43.510
Let me invite questions and comments to Arvind
from the board.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:44.510
Bruce?

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:47.050
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Thanks very much, Chairman.

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:54.750
Can I just perhaps put a little bit of context
around this, and indicate why we are doing

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.000
it?

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.320
Because you might think that most of this
of focussed on GPs, but the reality is by

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.861
focusing on general practice and primary care,
we bring benefits to our patients, and that

00:09:04.861 --> 00:09:07.760
is the endgame in all of this.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:15.970
Our GPs are dealing with increasingly complex
patients, their demands are going up at all

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:21.320
sorts of levels and they are functioning in
a very different societal environment to a

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:26.120
decade ago, or two decades ago.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:32.020
General practice has changed, and it seems
to me that one of the real challenges we have

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:38.450
is that the art and science of medicine come
together in a combined endeavour in general

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:44.760
practice, not just to treat individual diseases
but also to treat the families that are affected

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:50.240
by those diseases and the communities in which
those diseases occur.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:56.120
We are increasingly asking general practitioners
to become engaged in a prevention agenda,

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.730
which is clearly very important.

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:06.660
In real life, it seems to me that they have
to be on constant alert for minor ailments

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:13.640
that are masquerading as pretty serious diseases,
and there is a changing set of expectations

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:15.970
that they do not miss those diseases.

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:26.460
So, the end result is that general practice
is clinically, intellectually and emotionally

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:27.460
demanding.

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:32.450
It is emotionally demanding in part because
of the clinical environment, and also in part

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:38.510
because of the bureaucratic environment in
which they have to function.

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:42.110
So there is nothing kind of 'general' about
general practice.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:47.810
As patients have moved out of hospital to
be treated in the community, we have seen

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:53.640
a commensurate requirement for the professionalism
of general practitioners, who have increasing

00:10:53.640 --> 00:11:00.590
become the general physicians, the consultant
community physicians of the day, and that

00:11:00.590 --> 00:11:03.640
is a very important change in my view.

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:10.550
However, at the same time I think there is
evidence that our general practitioners have

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:16.580
been undervalued by the NHS at the time that
we need them most, when we are asking for

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:18.430
them to do more.

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:25.550
Their income has dropped very significantly,
in real terms, since 2005/06.

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:31.110
During a similar kind of period, the number
of consultations of increasingly complex patients

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:39.030
has gone up by 14%, and there are a set of
expectations which have accompanied that;

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:46.000
expectations which GPs have imposed on themselves
to ensure that they provide the best quality

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.550
clinical care that they can, but also a set
of expectations which come from patients and

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:55.610
those that oversee the NHS.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:12:01.580
Equally, as has been pointed out in the Five
Year Forward View, during a similar period

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:07.170
of time we have seen levels of investment
down by 0.8% annually in general practice,

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:16.070
whilst it has risen in hospitals by 12.3%,
and there seems to be an inequity in that.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:20.790
Much of this becomes reflected in the longitudinal
work life survey.

00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:27.450
We know that morale in general practice is
the lowest that it has been since 2001.

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:32.320
Then when you start to dissect that out, you
see that it is due to a feeling of excessive

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:38.460
workload; unresourced work which has been
moved out of hospitals into general practice;

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:42.770
not enough time with patients, which is really
important if you are going to engage in the

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:48.910
sort of relationships that you need to deliver
the kind of care I have described; and the

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:52.360
weight of regulation.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:57.980
This is also reflected if you look at the
proportion of people who are leaving general

00:12:57.980 --> 00:12:59.320
practice early, retiring early.

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:08.970
It is up 10% for the ages between 55 and 65
between 2004 and 2014, and that is a combination

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:17.940
of dissatisfaction with the working environment
and complicated further by pension considerations.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:24.910
On top of that, it has made it more difficult
to recruit people into general practice.

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:32.020
So, despite all of this, it is worth noting
that the general practice outcome standards

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:35.990
have improved over the last year in 30% of
practices.

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:41.790
So we cannot afford to keep on losing our
general practitioners, and we cannot afford

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:45.240
to have a disenchanted general practitioner
workforce.

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:49.990
That is what the Five Year Forward View is
about, and that is what the endeavours that

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:53.550
Arvind has outlined are about.

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:58.490
The reality is that the best way of looking
after our patients in the community is by

00:13:58.490 --> 00:14:03.930
looking after our general practitioners and
primary care, and that is true both for our

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:06.380
current patients and for our future patients.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:08.600
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, Bruce.

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:13.781
I mean, I really should have invited you to
have spoken in the different order, because

00:14:13.781 --> 00:14:17.230
you have laid the foundation for the very
issues that Arvind was addressing through

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:20.970
the general practice five year view.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:26.010
Then in a moment we will invite Ian to talk
about the MCP model and we get, I think, a

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:29.890
supplementary leg of our approach to the issues.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:36.360
Let me then invite members of the board to
raise issues with either Bruce or with Arvind.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.730
I have Moira and David.

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:39.730
Moira?

00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:40.730
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Thank you, Malcolm.

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:45.130
Just to say it is very encouraging that there
is such a coherent programme of investment

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:49.380
in what is obviously an incredibly important
part of the NHS.

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:56.910
I just wanted to ask, because it is a variety
of things: will we be able to track what is

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:00.060
making the difference?

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:06.570
Because, although you have referred to history
in terms of low morale, there is a kind of

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:13.190
cyclical process where it seems to me that
primary care gets abandoned and then focused

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:14.190
on again.

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:19.450
How will you know which of these things have
made more of a difference?

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:22.760
Secondly, really around the kind of scale
of the recruitment - Simon referred to the

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:30.500
Brexit unit within NHS England - does it begin
to match anything like the potential loss

00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:35.250
we might have through EU citizens not being
able to be here?

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:44.160
&gt;&gt;Arvind Madan: So, in relation to the first
question, around tracking: this is particularly

00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:48.941
key, because actually I think the majority
of the audience who view the General Practice

00:15:48.941 --> 00:15:55.200
Forward View, from the perspective of primary
care, would say, 'Actually, these are a laudable

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:59.630
set of commitments that are not particularly
contentious.'

00:15:59.630 --> 00:16:04.200
Most people would be in agreement with them.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:08.720
Where the issue becomes a little less clear
is, actually, are they really going to be

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.670
delivered and is it really going to have an
impact?

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:18.890
Actually, one of the things that we are looking
to do within the implementation group is actually

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:25.320
track the progress of each of the 80 initiatives
across each of the CCGs and give visibility

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:29.750
to that exercise to others within the system,
and other stakeholders who have an interest

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:35.530
in this space, to actually create, if you
like, a single version of the truth of actually

00:16:35.530 --> 00:16:39.850
how are we doing and actually is it making
a difference and is everyone in agreement

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:44.030
that the initiatives we think are happening
in particular areas are indeed happening,

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:48.300
and actually how do we unpack some of those
situations and look at them where there is

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:51.250
less agreement?

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:56.310
So actually, we believe that this will drive
some transparency through the system, some

00:16:56.310 --> 00:17:03.800
assurance, and actually the ability to create
the ability to learn from neighbours who are

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:06.589
actually having some success in particular
areas.

00:17:06.589 --> 00:17:11.980
We are not keen to make that a performance
management approach, we want to make that

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:16.189
a sort of supportive learning approach to
work out how we roll out something that we

00:17:16.189 --> 00:17:20.579
think is part of the right solution for general
practice.

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:25.679
We should stress that this is a turning point,
this is the beginning of an exercise, and

00:17:25.679 --> 00:17:31.190
actually the transformation that we are proposing
is pretty ambitious and pretty substantial

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:34.360
and will have implications for other parts
of the NHS too.

00:17:34.360 --> 00:17:38.320
However, it is a step in the right direction.

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:43.600
In relation to the area of recruitment, it
is a particularly challenging place when,

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:50.360
as Bruce has highlighted, morale is at its
lowest since records began in 2001, and statistics

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:56.620
around how many GPs are looking to leave the
profession in the next five years is somewhere

00:17:56.620 --> 00:18:03.210
around a third, through self reporting; that
jumps to somewhere around 60% over the age

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:04.210
of 50.

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:11.399
So we have quite a challenge hard wired into
the existing demography of the workforce.

00:18:11.399 --> 00:18:17.790
However, there are efforts being made around
recruitment, and Health Education England

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:24.440
have reported that they recruited more GPs
this year than ever before: the measures that

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:28.940
we are taking around workforce, around retainer
schemes, returner schemes, trying to reduce

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:35.630
the bureaucratic burden of re entering the
workforce after a break and the sort of mix

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:40.070
of portfolio options that we are going to
be rolling out for doctors towards the latter

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:41.070
end of their career.

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:47.379
So, for example, there may be some overlap
in how a GP who is looking to do fewer sessions

00:18:47.379 --> 00:18:51.590
towards the end of their career; we can lock
in that experience to the system by perhaps

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:57.970
having them work in resilience teams, and
deploy some of that experience to practices

00:18:57.970 --> 00:18:59.500
that may benefit.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:04.559
So there is a variety of things that are happening
that are hoping to turn that situation around,

00:19:04.559 --> 00:19:08.990
but we have the ambition of 5,000 additional
doctors in general practice by 2020/21.

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:11.789
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: David, and then Wendy.

00:19:11.789 --> 00:19:16.130
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Thank you Arvind, and thanks
for that presentation.

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:24.350
I think that this is at the heart of the change
that we need to effect the transformation

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:29.639
of primary care, so for me it is mission critical.

00:19:29.639 --> 00:19:35.440
So my question is less to you and more to
Paul and to Simon, actually, which is: underpinning

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:43.279
this programme is a significant increase in
the amount of money going into primary care

00:19:43.279 --> 00:19:48.450
over the next three, four, five years; well,
every year but over that period.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:56.379
Historically, primary care has suffered when
it has come to balancing the books, and so

00:19:56.379 --> 00:19:57.379
on.

00:19:57.379 --> 00:20:03.110
Therefore, I would just like maybe Simon or
Paul to maybe just tell us how you are thinking

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:08.059
about that, and the extent to which we can
be assured that the commitments that have

00:20:08.059 --> 00:20:13.720
been made will be matched by the money?

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:17.330
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: So the money that Arvind has
talked about is absolutely firmly ring fenced

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:20.820
within our budget; it is clearly stated in
the allocations that we have agreed upon;

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:21.909
it is proceeding.

00:20:21.909 --> 00:20:26.039
The allocations, where they have not already
gone out, will go out as soon as we are ready

00:20:26.039 --> 00:20:28.890
to confirm where they should go to.

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:32.860
So I think that we can be totally assured
that the transformation money for primary

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:34.990
care will go.

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:41.330
To be very clear, primary care, in its routine
budgets has set aside the 1% that is required

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:47.350
of all commissioners, and clearly that part
of it is dependent upon the overall performance

00:20:47.350 --> 00:20:48.350
of the system.

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:52.510
However, when I come to my financial report,
later on I will be trying to stress the actions

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:57.889
we are taking to make sure that that 1% too,
both for CCGs and primary care, is kept intact

00:20:57.889 --> 00:21:02.840
for doing transformational purposes, rather
than being used for offsetting purposes.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:07.019
I will come back to that, if I may, when I
do the finance report.

00:21:07.019 --> 00:21:08.499
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Okay.

00:21:08.499 --> 00:21:09.499
Simon?

00:21:09.499 --> 00:21:13.580
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, as I mentioned in my
written Chief Executive's Report -

00:21:13.580 --> 00:21:16.330
&gt;David Roberts: Yes, I was just trying to
decode it.

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:17.330
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Well, I mean it is not too
coded.

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:24.399
It sort of says we have been in a period where,
in effect, individual organisations, individual

00:21:24.399 --> 00:21:28.940
hospitals were choosing their deficit and
then expecting somebody else to pick up the

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:34.470
tab, given that we live in a nationally budgeted
system and parliament sets the amount of money

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:36.669
that the NHS, overall, gets to spend each
year.

00:21:36.669 --> 00:21:42.380
A consequence of that has been that somebody
else, namely mental health services and primary

00:21:42.380 --> 00:21:46.519
care, have been suffering as a direct effect.

00:21:46.519 --> 00:21:52.190
That is not right for patients, it is unfair
to staff in other parts of the system and

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:56.950
it is inconsistent with the requirement on
boards and managers to be effective stewards

00:21:56.950 --> 00:22:01.620
of the public resources that they are actually
in charge of.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:05.809
So we have to change that, and the word we
have used for that is 'reset'.

00:22:05.809 --> 00:22:08.210
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good, thank you very much.

00:22:08.210 --> 00:22:09.210
Wendy?

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:13.340
&gt;&gt;Wendy Becker: I would like to just echo
my colleague's congratulations; I think it

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:18.009
is a very exciting paper and the breadth of
the programme is outstanding.

00:22:18.009 --> 00:22:23.690
The question I had: I was very encouraged
by the number of people providing oversight

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:27.120
and the fact that we are continuing to bring
in other groups to support us in this, and

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:31.529
I was very encouraged by the ambassador programme
that has been laid out, because I think it

00:22:31.529 --> 00:22:36.240
is so important that the voice of the GPs
comes to the programme, as well as the other

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:37.470
KPI tracking.

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:41.440
How will their voice be incorporated in, and
how will we continue to learn from feedback

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:43.990
from this important population?

00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:51.029
&gt;&gt;Arvind Madan: Well, the Royal College of
GPs, as I have said, has announced 29, and

00:22:51.029 --> 00:22:56.179
I think they are looking to employ another
four or five, so that they have full coverage.

00:22:56.179 --> 00:23:01.510
Actually we have been involved in their induction
programme, to ensure that we bring them up

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:05.360
that learning curve as quickly as possible
in understanding the sort of architecture

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:10.279
of the system, and where they may have the
ability to input and have influence.

00:23:10.279 --> 00:23:16.679
We have made direct introductions to our directors
of commissioning operations in each of the

00:23:16.679 --> 00:23:22.529
regions, so they are connected with the NHS
England teams quite quickly, and we have made

00:23:22.529 --> 00:23:29.929
introductions for them to join the teams that
are developing local sustainability and transformation

00:23:29.929 --> 00:23:30.929
plans.

00:23:30.929 --> 00:23:38.530
They are also key participants in some of
the events that we are holding nationally,

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:44.090
around the country, to raise awareness of
all the moving parts of the General Practice

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:50.690
Forward View and actually become the sort
of local point of contact for a lot of GPs

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:56.200
in those areas, as an information source for
the queries they have around it.

00:23:56.200 --> 00:24:01.710
So we very much want to bed them into the
structure, and we are really grateful to the

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:07.470
Royal College of GPs in terms of making a
step of this level in terms of its commitment

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:08.559
to this direction.

00:24:08.559 --> 00:24:10.779
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Jane?

00:24:10.779 --> 00:24:13.740
&gt;&gt;Jane Cummings: Thanks, Chair.

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:16.139
Great paper and thank you very much.

00:24:16.139 --> 00:24:19.990
One of the things, for me, that is really
positive about this is that it is about a

00:24:19.990 --> 00:24:25.909
general practice five year forward view, which
encompasses the whole of the team, as it were.

00:24:25.909 --> 00:24:30.799
I think Bruce has very clearly outlined why
it is so important that we provide the additional

00:24:30.799 --> 00:24:33.629
support for GPs, and I think that is absolutely
right.

00:24:33.629 --> 00:24:37.529
However, I just wanted to flag - and I think
most people will be aware - that the team

00:24:37.529 --> 00:24:40.009
is obviously wider than GPs.

00:24:40.009 --> 00:24:44.890
What is really positive for me with the Forward
View is that it does talk about the wider

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:49.610
team, it recognises the role that other healthcare
professionals, like general practice nurses,

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:54.350
play, and there are some specific actions
in there to enhance that role, to talk about

00:24:54.350 --> 00:25:00.600
return to practice and, more importantly for
me, actually look at pre registration clinical

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:02.539
placements for student nurses.

00:25:02.539 --> 00:25:05.840
So that what we are talking about is this
is actually a place that people want to work,

00:25:05.840 --> 00:25:09.379
they want to do that; that is where they see
their future career.

00:25:09.379 --> 00:25:14.759
Because the opportunities to support patients
in primary care settings in their own homes

00:25:14.759 --> 00:25:17.720
is absolutely phenomenal, and we really should
be encouraging that.

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:23.110
So I just really wanted to emphasise that
is a General Practice Forward View and something

00:25:23.110 --> 00:25:25.610
that the whole team are an essential part
of.

00:25:25.610 --> 00:25:28.330
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good, well thank you very
much.

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:31.710
Arvind, can I just, on behalf of the board,
say thank you again for the paper; I know

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:34.610
you have worked closely with Ros Roughton
on this.

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:43.809
I think the board is dead keen to continue
to track the progress of rolling forward the

00:25:43.809 --> 00:25:49.019
General Practice Forward View, so your assurance
on the tracking mechanisms I think is very

00:25:49.019 --> 00:25:50.019
welcome.

00:25:50.019 --> 00:25:53.999
I would commend also the very close working
with the Royal College of General Practitioners.

00:25:53.999 --> 00:25:59.019
I think as an overall demonstration of our
seriousness about general practice, this is

00:25:59.019 --> 00:26:02.999
a critical partnership that is worth building
on and developing.

00:26:02.999 --> 00:26:05.504
Picking up Jane's point that it is not just
GPs that we are talking about, we are talking

00:26:05.504 --> 00:26:12.440
about the whole team that deliver general
practice, I think we would like to have further

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:16.799
reports coming through over the coming year
to ensure that we are on target with what

00:26:16.799 --> 00:26:21.860
we are trying to achieve, and picking up on
all those points that were so well made by

00:26:21.860 --> 00:26:26.229
Bruce as to the fundamental importance of
general practice and what we seek to deliver.

